\section{Identity is not presentation}{


A comparison key is derived from the URL's canonical identity state -- after
the selected standard has interpreted it, and \emph{before} any cleaning or
display transform. No cleaning option can reach it. \code{www_handling},
\code{case_handling}, \code{trailing_slash_handling}, \code{index_page_handling},
\code{path_encoding}, \code{host_encoding}, \code{port_handling}, query cleaning and every
\code{\link[=url_profile]{url_profile()}} bundle are structurally incapable of changing a key byte.
That is the point: two URLs that a cleaning profile happens to render alike
are not thereby the same resource.
}

\section{What the key does and does not distinguish}{


Framed as identity: the scheme (and, separately, whether one was written at
all), the authority delimiter, the host and its kind, the port, the path and
its kind, and the query -- order and duplicates significant.

Excluded by contract: the fragment and any userinfo. Neither identifies a
web resource, so \verb{http://u:pw@h/p#frag} and \verb{http://h/p} mint the same key.
Their structural state is still available from \code{\link[=safe_parse_url]{safe_parse_url()}} and the
diagnostics helpers.

Ports normalize only where the standard makes them redundant: an explicit
\verb{:80} under \code{http} and \verb{:443} under \code{https} compare equal to no port at all.
Every other default stays literal, so \verb{ftp://h:21/} and \verb{ftp://h/} are
distinct, and an inferred scheme normalizes nothing (\verb{h.com:80/} is not
\verb{http://h.com/}).
}

\section{Versioning}{


The policy carries a key version and a schema version, and both travel
\emph{inside} the framed key bytes. A key minted under different semantics can
therefore never compare equal to one minted here, so no release can silently
reinterpret a persisted key.
}

